{{Infobox food|name=Mee ka tee|image=Mee ka tee (Laotian egg curry noodle soup).jpg|image_size=250px|caption=|alternate_name=''mee katee Lao'', ''mee kati Vientiane'', ''mee gati''|country=[[Laos]]|region=|creator=|course=|type=[[Soup]]|served=|main_ingredient=[[Rice noodles]], [[coconut milk]], [[Eggs as food|egg]], [[curry]]|variations=|calories=|other=|similar_dish=[[Khao poon]]}}

'''''Mee ka tee''''' ({{langx|lo|ໝີ່ກະທິ}}), also known as '''''mee ka tee Lao''''', '''''mee ka tee Vientiane''''', '''''Laotian pork curry noodle soup''''', '''''mee kati''''', '''''mee kathi''''' or '''''mee gati''''', is a Laotian [[red curry]] and coconut milk [[Egg drop soup|egg drop]] [[noodle soup]]<ref>{{Cite web|last=TOMETICH|first=ANNABELLE|title=Hot Dish: At Thai Star, a Lao soup with exotic appeal|url=https://www.news-press.com/story/life/food/2014/10/08/hot-dish-thai-star-lao-soup-exotic-appeal/16879741/|access-date=2021-07-04|website=The News-Press|language=en-US}}</ref> originating from [[Vientiane]], [[Laos]].<ref name="Noi2024">{{cite book |last1=Kaewduangdee |first1=Ponpailin "Noi" |last2=Shippen |first2=Mick |title=A Child of the Rice Fields: Recipes from Noi’s Lao Kitchen |date=2024 |publisher=Doi Ka Noi |location=Vientiane |page=312}}</ref> ''Mee ka tee'' is a noodle soup consisting of [[rice noodles]] in broth with coconut cream, hand-pounded red curry paste, ground (minced) pork, peanuts and egg, topped with fresh shredded cabbage, [[cilantro]], green onions, [[beansprout]]s and mint.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-01-06|title=Mee Katee - Egg Red Curry Noodle Soup|url=https://saengskitchen.com/2020/01/06/mee-katee-egg-red-curry-noodle-soup/|access-date=2021-07-04|website=saeng's kitchen|language=en-US}}</ref> Sometimes ''mee ka tee'' is served with [[Glutinous_rice#Laos|Laotian puffed sticky rice]] and deep-fried sun-dried peppers.

''Mee ka tee'' in the Lao language literally means "''coconut milk noodles''". ''Mee'' means 'noodle', and ''ka tee'' means '''coconut cream/milk''<nowiki/>'. It is said to be the sweeter and creamier cousin of ''[[khao poon]]'', or even a soupy version of ''[[pad Thai]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Oodles Page 3|url=https://www.laosinthehouse.com/oodles-page-3|access-date=2021-07-03|website=Laos In The House|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2019-03-13|title=One of the most delicious things I've eaten in Dallas came from Khao Noodle Shop|url=https://www.dallasnews.com/food/restaurant-reviews/2019/03/13/one-of-the-most-delicious-things-i-ve-eaten-in-dallas-came-from-khao-noodle-shop/|access-date=2021-07-04|website=Dallas News|language=en}}</ref>
